ows that two thirds of the vertices of Ψ(r)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSix Families of Binary Codes Arising from Ding's Conjectures (https://www.newx.sg/paper/detail/e0d7cdae-bd7e-11f1-b84c-00163e10baa7)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; # Six Families of Binary Codes Arising from Ding's Conjectures ... For Conjecture~30, we prove that every admissible code has three, four, or five nonzero weights, and an explicit four-weight example disproves the original ``three or five weights'' assertion. ... For Conjecture~34, we obtain a general $(2h+1)$-weight upper bound and give an explicit six-weight counterexample, showing that the original ``three or five weights'' assertion is false in general, where $h$ is a positive integer.\n\n# Six Families of Binary Codes Arising from Ding's Conjectures\n\n📅 2026-09-29\n\n📈 Citations: 0\n\n✨ Influential: 0\n\n📄 PDF♡ Bookmark📋 Share\n\n🤖 AI Summary\n\nThis study addresses the six remaining open conjectures proposed by Ding concerning binary linear codes derived from Boolean functions. By integrating combinatorics, finite field theory, and Boolean function analysis, we systematically investigate the weight distribution properties of specific families of binary codes. Through the derivation of general upper bounds on weights and the construction of counterexamples to refine the original conjectures, we precisely determine several parameter cases. The principal contributions include confirming or refuting the relevant conjectures, discovering an infinite family of five-weight codes, and completely resolving Conjecture 37. These findings provide essential theoretical support for advancing research in coding theory.\n\n📝 Abstract\n\nDing \\cite{Ding2016} proposed ten conjectures on binary linear codes arising from Boolean functions. Four of them, namely Conjectures 38--41, were subsequently proved by Göloğlu and Krasnayová \\cite{GologluKrasnayova2019}. In this paper, we investigate the remaining six conjectures, namely Conjectures 19, 27, 30, 33, 34, and 37. For Conjectures~19 and~27, we obtain common weight restrictions and several infinite five-weight families. For Conjecture~30, we prove that every admissible code has three, four, or five nonzero weights, and an explicit four-weight example disproves the original ``three or five weights'' assertion. For Conjecture~33, an infinite five-weight family is obtained. For Conjecture~34, we obtain a general $(2h+1)$-weight upper bound and give an explicit six-weight counterexample, showing that the original ``three or five weights'' assertion is false in general, where $h$ is a positive integer. The case $h=3$ with $3\\nmid m$ is also completely determined. Finally, Conjecture~37 is completely resolved by combining the known results of Ahmadi and Shafaeiabr \\cite{AhmadiShafaeiabr2023} with the treatment of the two remaining classes $(a)$ and $(b)$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll works · Hexagon (https://hexagonmath.org/search?per_page=100)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; As an application, the number $D_n$D n​ of representations of $n$n by the ternary form $8x^2 + 2y^2 + 16z^2$8 x 2+2 y 2+16 z 2 in Tunnell's congruent number criterion equals $2h(-4n)$2 h(−4 n) for square-free $n \\equiv 2 \\pmod 8$n≡2(mod 8) and vanishes for square-free $n \\equiv 6 \\pmod 8$n≡6(mod 8); hence $D_n$D n​ lies in $\\text{FP}^{\\Sigma_2^{\\text{P}}}$FP Σ 2 P​ on even square-free $n$n, and an exact counting reduction from $\\#\\text{SAT}$#SAT to $D_n$D n​ would collapse the polynomial hierarchy to $\\Delta_3^{\\text{P}}$Δ 3 P​. ... Assuming GRH for Dedekind zeta functions, we prove Serre’s conjecture over the real quadratic fields $\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt d)$Q(d​) for \\[ d\\in\\{2,3,5,6,7,10,11,13,17,21,33,37,41,57,65,73,89\\}. ... ### The binary Collatz dynamics of the trinomials x^n+x+1: an independent proof of two conjectures of Gallardo and Rahavandrainy ... They proved that every orbit reaches 1, and conjectured that the orbit of the trinomial x^n+x+1 has length 2^s+1, where s is the largest integer with 2^(s+1) < n, and that for 1 <= t <= s-1 it contains 2^t terms of the same degree.\n\nPrimarily AI-generated text Human understanding: no parts Hankel determinants automatic sequences continued fractions power series over finite fields\n\nNov 03, 2026PDF ↗View work →\n\nhexagon:2611.00031v2\n\nmath.NT — Number Theory\n\n### The binary Collatz dynamics of the trinomials x^n+x+1: an independent proof of two conjectures of Gallardo and Rahavandrainy\n\nContributed by David Niedbala Giraudin\n\nGallardo and Rahavandrainy studied an analogue of the Collatz map on F_2[x], in which x(x+1) plays the role of 2 and x^2+x+1 that of 3: an odd polynomial A is sent to the odd part of 1+(x^2+x+1)A. They proved that every orbit reaches 1, and conjectured that the orbit of the trinomial x^n+x+1 has length 2^s+1, where s is the largest integer with 2^(s+1) < n, and that for 1 <= t <= s-1 it contains 2^t terms of the same degree. We prove both statements for every n >= 3. They were proved earlier, with a complete Lean 4 formalization, by W. Choi; the proof given here was obtained independently. The proof rests on the identity (x^2+x+1)+x(x+1)=1: it reduces the dynamics to a map from one plateau to the next, characterises the last plateau of any orbit, and shows that along the orbit of x^n+x+1 the plateaus start at the 2^j-th powers of the odd parts of the polynomials x^m(x^2+x+1)+1, the index m following an explicit binary recursion. We also note that a later restatement of the conjecture, with a non-strict inequality, fails exactly when n is a power of 2. A script checks all the statements numerically.\n\nPrimarily AI-generated text Human understanding: no parts\n\nNov 03, 2026PDF ↗View work →\n\nhexagon:2611.00030v1\n\nmath.NT — Number Theory\n\n---------------------------------