 every non-identity element g of G the number of (x, y)\n\nsolutions of the equation g=xy−1 with x, y ∈ D is exactly λ.\n\nDiﬀerence sets have many applications in the construction of combinatorial designs,\n\nerror correcting codes, sequences with good correlation properties as well as the study of\n\nﬁnite geometries and cryptographic functions.\n\nAs it is the case with the construction of many other combinatorial objects, the theory\n\nof ﬁnite ﬁelds is of great importance in the construction of diﬀerence sets. Cyclic diﬀerence\n\nsets of special interest which are constructed from ﬁnite ﬁelds are the so called diﬀerence\n\nsets with Singer parameters for which either\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1−1\n\nq−1, λ =q n−2−1\n\nq−1\n\nor\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1, λ =q n−2(q−1)\n\nwhere q is a prime power and n≥3.\n\nIn this paper, we are interested in the construction of cyclic diﬀerence sets and linear\n\ncodes from polynomials and rational functions deﬁned over the binary ﬁnite ﬁeld F 2 m with\n\n2 m elements where m is an odd number. Our main result is the following theorem stated\n\nas Conjecture 36 in [5].\n\nTheorem 1. Let m≥5 be an odd number, F 2 m denote the degree-m extension of the\n\nbinary ﬁeld F 2, and F 2 m[x]denote the univariate polynomial ring over F 2 m. Furthermore,\n\nlet F∗\n\n2 m denote the multiplicative subgroup of F 2 m, and for a given f∈F 2 m[x],D(f)∗\n\ndenotes the punctured value-set it, that is,\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReferences - Boolean Functions for Cryptography and Coding Theory (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/boolean-functions-for-cryptography-and-coding-theory/references/35A2258FF424D1EB598B94275BD18BAB)\nciteturn8search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Cyclic codes from some monomials and trinomials. ... [458] Ding, C., Mesnager, S., Tang, C., and Xiong, M.. Cyclic bent functions and their applications in codes, codebooks, desig